ot the root alias. Two distinct paths that are hard links to one inode (e.g. a photo hard-linked into two album folders) were two rows at base. At head they are one row, and the other path cannot be picked. Codified as the code behaves: 9H5Y's rationale and hazard review (edge_case) and REVIEW-261002-VZ1K state it. Still only a question: the PR states the intent for aliased roots only, no upstream text says hiding a hard-linked path is wanted, and no test runs a hard link. The device:inode key is the fix our earlier GitHub review on this PR suggested; the PR adopted it at 03e6af6a.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: is it intended that hard-linked files collapse to one picker row?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Hard-linked files under one root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file","bin/omarchy-transcode"],"scenario":"omarchy-transcode with ~/Videos a symlink to a tree containing a mode-000 subdirectory: the user picks a file, and omarchy-menu-file exits 1 under pipefail so omarchy-transcode aborts"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Under `set -euo pipefail` the pipeline status is the rightmost non-zero one. `find -H ... 2>/dev/null` exits 1 on Permission denied but still prints the readable rows. The selector returns the pick, the script exits 1, and `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` (`input=$(omarchy-menu-file ...)` under `set -e`) aborts after the pick. This already happens for a real-directory root at base. -H makes it reachable through link roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/disk` with a root-owned `lost+found`), which listed nothing at base. Not codified as a guarantee: FFTQ's hazard review and REVIEW-261002-6P46 name it explicitly as open and not covered by the requirement. The PR's interior-loop row covers loops, not permissions.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: can a pick succeed and the script still exit 1 when a followed symlink root contains an unreadable directory?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"two files with identical mtime under one root, and aliased roots Pictures->Videos: compare row order and the surviving path spelling at base and head"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Base `sort -rn` reverses the whole-line tiebreak, so equal-mtime rows were ordered by path descending. Head `sort -t$'\\t' -k2,2rn` makes `r` apply to the key only, so ties fall back to an ascending whole-line comparison that starts with `D:i`. Re-checked with GNU sort on synthetic rows: `9:1 Pictures/b` is kept over `9:1 Videos/b`, and base orders `/r/b` before `/r/a`. The full find pipeline was not run (no GNU find on this host). Partly codified: REVIEW-261002-VZ1K says the lexically smaller line is kept, and 9H5Y's hazard review says the spelling depends on the sort order of equal rows. HR29 states mtime-descending only. No test pins the spelling the picker returns or the tie order.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: which alias spelling survives the dedup, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$tmp/link.jpg\" \"jpg\" where link.jpg is a symlink to a real jpg: base offers no row, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`-H` dereferences every start point. A root that is a link to a regular file with a matching extension was `-type l` at base (not listed). At head it is `-type f` and listed as one row. `[[ -e $path ]]` accepts it either way. Codified as the code behaves in FFTQ's hazard review (edge_case). FFTQ's description still speaks of links to directories only. The PR describes directory roots only. No test covers it.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":32},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed now?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Symlink-to-file root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"review.compat is `compatible`: test_diff, mcdc_diff (85 -> 87 decisions), removed_behavior and differential were all checked, with 0 findings and 213 inputs over 5 partitions. The configured harness `test/bdiff/harness.mjs` covers only `shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js` and `Menu.qml`, so none of those inputs ran this script. `review.behaviorDiff.inventory` is empty. The removed expressions (`find` default -P, `-printf '%T@\\t%p\\n'`, `sort -rn`, `cut -f2-`) were never run base-vs-head; the hazards above are what such a run would settle. test_diff reports no existing test changed. The edits to `menu-file-test.sh` (our proof work) only add rows and widen its Verifies line. Context, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":47},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"file"}],"severity":"info","title":"Compatibility 'compatible' carries no differential evidence for bin/omarchy-menu-file","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`coverage_unmeasured`: `review.diffCoverage.unmeasuredFiles` = [bin/omarchy-menu-file]. The 0 uncovered lines/outcomes says nothing about lines 47-56. The bash coverage target for this script, `menu-bash-file` (proof.yaml:501), is `enabled: false`, so no artifact measures it. `effects_unavailable`: `review.effects` is absent (call-level effects are not analyzed for bash), so no new-call, gained-effect or caller facts exist. Callers found by reading: `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` and `bin/omarchy-games-retro-install:22` (`|| exit 0`).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":501},{"id":"bin/omarchy-games-retro-install","kind":"file","line":22}],"severity":"info","title":"Changed-code coverage of the script was not measured; effects were not analyzed","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The script's two self-skips (`no GNU find`, `find lacks -printf`) call base-test.sh `pass` (not `skip`) and exit 0. The JUnit testcase would therefore record a pass with nothing run. On this run the evidence points to a real run. `menu-file-test.sh` sits in the same suite behind a GNU find -printf gate that records a JUnit skip (proof.yaml:200), and it ran and passed: testResults show 56/56 passed, 0 skipped. Its new witness rows independently exercise FFTQ and 9H5Y. Upstream