e10d767b9333492c91665762e40a0708c371c6a5f6a495b6b193c020a46ba","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A small, well-motivated pipeline change. Its purpose is now codified (FFTQ, 9H5Y) and observed passing, with witnesses that fail on the base. Coverage of the script was not measured, effects were not analyzed, and the differential harness does not run the script. Untested edges remain: hard-link collapse, pipefail exit 1 via an unreadable directory under a followed root (named open in FFTQ), tie order and alias spelling, symlink-to-file roots."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":4,"rationale":"Settles whether %D:%i dedup should drop a distinct, selectable path; record the decision in 9H5Y or the code.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Hard-linked files under one root"},{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"mkdir a mode-000 dir under the link target (run as non-root), pick a file with a stub selector, and assert omarchy-menu-file's exit status (and omarchy-transcode's).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169}],"title":"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"touch -d the same time on two files and on an aliased pair, then assert row order and which path the stub selector receives, at base and head.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling"},{"forClaim":7,"rationale":"Pass a symlink to a matching file as the path argument; assert whether it is offered.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54}],"title":"Symlink-to-file root"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #13197 (Bartok9, part of #12665) makes `bin/omarchy-menu-file` list files under symlinked roots, so `omarchy transcode` gets a picker when `~/Videos` is a link. It runs `find -H` (start points followed, links inside a root not descended), prints `%D:%i`, and keeps one row per device:inode after the mtime sort. It adds `test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh` (8 rows). Evaluated commit c77bb16d is baseline quattro-proof 41076fd4 plus the upstream change (82c08ee4, head 03e6af6a) and our proof work (213192c3..a069b9e7): SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ and SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y under SYS-REQ-260922-6642, MC/DC and error_handling witnesses in `menu-file-test.sh`, reviews REVIEW-261002-6P46/-VZ1K, and a `# Verifies:` line on the PR's test. The PR body is stale: it still describes a path-string dedup.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** The package says readiness `ready`; the next decision is `human_judgement` (acceptance) by a maintainer. Disclosures: 3, all inform (coverage_unmeasured, checks_skipped, effects_unavailable). FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29 are `observed_pass`; their error_handling cells are `observed_pass`; HR29's external_call_timeout_bounded is `not_linked` (accepted KI debt). testResults: 56 passed, 0 failed, current. Coverage of the script was not measured (its coverage target is disabled in proof.yaml) and effects were not analyzed. Compatibility is `compatible` (213 inputs, 0 findings), but the differential harness never runs this script.\n\n**Questions for a human.** Is it intended that hard links collapse to one row? Can a pick under a followed root holding an unreadable directory still exit 1 under pipefail? Which alias spelling survives, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted? Is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed? The requirements record the first, third and fourth as the code behaves and name the second as open. No test exercises any of them. The device:inode key matches the fix our earlier GitHub review suggested, so the hard-link question follows from our own suggestion.\n\n**Traceability.** The script and both tests trace to FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29. The package raises no spec-gap candidates and scope coherence is `single`.\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** 13 checks skipped by configuration; the GNU find dependency; the HR29 timeout debt (KI-MENU-SELECT-POLL-DEADLOCK). Any auditor assessment recorded on this package is advisory, not an approval."}
